Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  

bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_committee_2016  
• `cmte_id`  : committee identifier – link to both contributions and committee‐name tables  
• `cand_id`  : candidate identifier – link to candidate_2016 for candidate names  
• `fec_election_yr` : keep only 2016 links  
• `cmte_tp / cmte_design` : not strictly needed here, but can be used to filter for (P, H, S …) committees if desired  

bigquery-public-data.fec.indiv16  
• `cmte_id`  : committee that received the contribution – join key  
• `transaction_amt` : contribution amount – filter for 0 < amount < 200 and sum per committee  
• (all other columns merely give detail about each contribution – not needed for the requested list)  

bigquery-public-data.fec.committee_2016  
• `cmte_id`  : join key  
• `cmte_nm`  : requested committee name to display  

bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_2016  
• `cand_id`  : join key  
• `cand_name` : requested candidate names to display (alphabetical list)  

Typical conditions we will need  

• On candidate‑committee link: `fec_election_yr = 2016`  
• On contributions: `transaction_amt > 0 AND transaction_amt < 200`  
• On aggregate small‑dollar sum: `small_dollar_total > 0`  

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  
Ten exploratory → final BigQuery SQL statements  
(Each query limited to 20 rows so they are inexpensive to run; every query is wrapped in back‑ticks and preceded by a brief description.)

```sql
-- 1. Peek at the candidate‑committee linkage table (2016 only)
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_committee_2016`
WHERE fec_election_yr = 2016
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Show distinct committees that supported at least one candidate in 2016
SELECT DISTINCT cmte_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_committee_2016`
WHERE fec_election_yr = 2016
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect a few individual‑contribution rows (all years) just to see the layout
SELECT cmte_id, transaction_dt, transaction_amt
FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.indiv16`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Verify how many rows qualify as “small‑dollar” contributions (0 < amt < 200)
SELECT COUNT(*) AS small_dollar_rows
FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.indiv16`
WHERE transaction_amt > 0 AND transaction_amt < 200;
```

```sql
-- 5. Aggregate small‑dollar totals per committee (no link to candidates yet)
SELECT
  cmte_id,
  SUM(transaction_amt) AS small_dollar_total
FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.indiv16`
WHERE transaction_amt > 0 AND transaction_amt < 200
GROUP BY cmte_id
HAVING small_dollar_total > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Bring in only those committees that actually supported a candidate in 2016
SELECT DISTINCT s.cmte_id
FROM (
  SELECT
    cmte_id,
    SUM(transaction_amt) AS small_dollar_total
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.indiv16`
  WHERE transaction_amt > 0 AND transaction_amt < 200
  GROUP BY cmte_id
  HAVING small_dollar_total > 0
) AS s
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_committee_2016` AS cc
ON s.cmte_id = cc.cmte_id
WHERE cc.fec_election_yr = 2016
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Count how many unique 2016 candidates each qualifying committee supported
SELECT
  cc.cmte_id,
  COUNT(DISTINCT cc.cand_id) AS candidate_count
FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_committee_2016` AS cc
JOIN (
  SELECT cmte_id
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.indiv16`
  WHERE transaction_amt > 0 AND transaction_amt < 200
) AS sm USING (cmte_id)
WHERE cc.fec_election_yr = 2016
GROUP BY cc.cmte_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Pull in candidate names and aggregate alphabetically for a single sample committee
SELECT
  cc.cmte_id,
  STRING_AGG(DISTINCT cand.cand_name, ', ' ORDER BY cand.cand_name) AS candidates_supported
FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_committee_2016` AS cc
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_2016` AS cand
  ON cand.cand_id = cc.cand_id
WHERE cc.cmte_id = 'C00188888'       -- sample id, replace to explore
GROUP BY cc.cmte_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Attach the committee names to the small‑dollar totals (quick sanity check)
SELECT
  com.cmte_nm,
  sm.small_dollar_total
FROM (
  SELECT
    cmte_id,
    SUM(transaction_amt) AS small_dollar_total
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.indiv16`
  WHERE transaction_amt > 0 AND transaction_amt < 200
  GROUP BY cmte_id
  HAVING small_dollar_total > 0
) AS sm
LEFT JOIN `bigquery-public-data.fec.committee_2016` AS com
ON sm.cmte_id = com.cmte_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER QUERY:
-- Committees that (a) supported ≥1 candidate in 2016, (b) took in >$0 small‑dollar contributions (<$200 each).
SELECT
  com.cmte_nm                                   AS committee_name,
  COUNT(DISTINCT cc.cand_id)                    AS num_unique_candidates,
  STRING_AGG(DISTINCT cand.cand_name,
             ', ' ORDER BY cand.cand_name)      AS candidate_names_alpha,
  ROUND(SUM(sm.transaction_amt), 2)             AS small_dollar_total
FROM `bigquery-public-data.fec.indiv16` AS sm
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_committee_2016` AS cc
  ON sm.cmte_id = cc.cmte_id
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.fec.committee_2016`      AS com
  ON com.cmte_id = cc.cmte_id
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.fec.candidate_2016`      AS cand
  ON cand.cand_id = cc.cand_id
WHERE
  cc.fec_election_yr = 2016
  AND sm.transaction_amt > 0
  AND sm.transaction_amt < 200
GROUP BY com.cmte_nm
HAVING
  small_dollar_total > 0
ORDER BY small_dollar_total DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

Each query builds on the previous one, moving from simple exploratory steps to a complete statement that will return exactly the information requested.
